  {
    "@id": "https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Build/buildType",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A buildType is a hint that is used to indicate the toolchain, platform, or\ninfrastructure that the build was invoked on."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Build/"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "buildType"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range": [
      {
        "@id": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A buildType is a hint that is used to indicate the toolchain, platform, or\ninfrastructure that the build was invoked on."
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A buildType is an IRI expressing the toolchain, platform, or infrastructure that\nthe build was invoked on.\n\nThe buildType is used to interpret the meaning of other build parameters by\ndefining the \"type\" of build; if the same buildType is seen in different Build\nelements, it means they are the same kind of build, but difference instances\nand possible with different configurations.\n\nIf you are not using a well-known buildType, it should be namespaced to a\ndomain you own to prevent conflicts with other buildType IRIs.\n\nExamples of a buildType can be:\n\n- A GitHub action workflow\n- A step in a GitHub actions pipeline\n- An invocation of a compiler or other tool\n- A script that orchestrates builds at a higher level\n\nKeep in mind that builds can be \"nested\" using the ancestorOf relationship.\n\nIf the buildType IRI is not recognized, it is still possible to inspect other\nproperties of the build, but it may not be possible to derive deeper meaning\nfrom them.\n\nFor more information, see the SLSA definition of buildType."
      }
    ]
  },
